1. Goles and Olivos, *Periodic behaviour of generalized threshold
   functions*, Discrete Mathematics 30(2), 187–189 (1980), DOI
   `10.1016/0012-365X(80)90121-1`. The publisher-indexed abstract actually
   read states period at most two for symmetric binary threshold maps.
   Direct publisher open returned `Internal Error`; no full article-body
   read is claimed. SRT's exact adapter is proved locally, and a negative
   mechanism disposition does not depend on pretending that access succeeded.
2. Durand, Formenti and Róka, *Number conserving cellular automata: from
   decidability to dynamics*, arXiv `nlin/0102035v1` (2001),
   <https://arxiv.org/html/nlin/0102035>. Actual body read used here is the
   introduction and definitions, browser lines 28–79: number conservation
   on periodic configurations and the established finite-alphabet CA
   setting. GCF0 is instead given on fixed-mass finite rings and need not
   preserve an arbitrary fixed-alphabet full shift. This source is background
   for conservation vocabulary, not an owner theorem for our gcd current or
   a proof of GCF0 convergence.
3. Fukś and Jin's 2026 arXiv `2601.00479` record was a topical discovery
   lead. Its four-input binary blocking-word map is not a claimed GCF0
   adapter. The returned HTML was not used to import a theorem.

No exact direct primary owner for GCF0 was obtained by the bounded three
gcd-current query formulations. No-hit and noisy unrelated hits have zero
novelty weight. A full direct-owner gate remains open. No secondary forum,
search snippet, metadata-only claim or empty result is promoted to a proof.
Mathematical-source fitness is judged at the stated theorem/body scope;
medical experimental evidence levels and unrelated ML venue quotas are not
used to inflate confidence. This is AI-assisted author scouting, not
independent specialist review or an external acceptance.
